    "q-79814": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79814",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "If $f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{x}+xz,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(0,1,2)$ to $(-1,0,3).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(y{e}^{x}+z)\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y+x\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,1,2),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0,1,2) & =(1\\cdot {e}^{0}+2)\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y+0\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+0\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,1+Δy,2+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈3\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0,1,2)$ to $(-1,0,3),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =-1-0=-1, \\\\ Δy & =0-1=-1 \\\\ Δz & =3-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈3\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ decreases by approximately $-4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
