    "q-167166": {
      "dom_id": "step-q167166",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "If $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{2y-z},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(-1,2,3)$ to $(1,0,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}x+2x{e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}y-x{e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(-1,2,3),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(-1,2,3) & ={e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot {e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}y-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =e\\,\\text{d}x-2e\\,\\text{d}y+e\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(-1+Δx,2+Δy,3+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈e\\,Δx-2e\\,Δy+e\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(-1,2,3)$ to $(1,0,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-(-1)=2, \\\\ Δy & =0-2=-2 \\\\ Δz & =1-3=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈e\\,Δx-2e\\,Δy+e\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈e\\cdot (2)-2e\\cdot (-2)+e\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & ≈4e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $4e.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
