      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "Estimate $\\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}$ at the point $(0,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1}=f(0.2,0.1),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(0.2,0.1).$\n\nMoving from $(0,0)$ to $(0.2,0.1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0.2-0=0.2, \\\\ Δy & =0.1-0=0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)+\\frac{1}{2}(0.1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.05 \\\\  & =0.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.15.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.2,0.1) & ≈f(0,0)+0.15 \\\\ \\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1} & ≈\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}+0.15 \\\\  & =1+0.15 \\\\  & =1.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
