    "q-79837": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79837",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Estimate $(1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ at the point $(2,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+2\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n(1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91)=f(1.81,0.91),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1.81,0.91).$\n\nMoving from $(2,1)$ to $(1.81,0.91),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.81-2=-0.19, \\\\ Δy & =0.91-1=-0.09.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\cdot (-0.19)+2\\cdot (-0.09) \\\\  & ≈0-0.18 \\\\  & ≈-0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.18.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.81,0.91) & ≈f(2,1)-0.18 \\\\ (1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91) & ≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-0.18 \\\\  & ≈0-0.18 \\\\  & ≈-0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
