    "q-79839": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79839",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "Estimate the value of ${e}^{\\sqrt{10}-3.2}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{x}-y}$ at the point $(9,3).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x}-y}}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x-{e}^{\\sqrt{x}-y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(9,3),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{9}-3}}{2\\sqrt{9}}\\,\\text{d}x-{e}^{\\sqrt{9}-3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{6}\\,\\text{d}x-{e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x-1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(9+Δx,3+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{6}\\,Δx-1\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n{e}^{\\sqrt{10}-3.2}=f(10,3.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(10,3.2).$\n\nMoving from $(9,3)$ to $(10,3.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =10-9=1, \\\\ Δy & =3.2-3=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & ≈0.167-0.2 \\\\  & ≈-0.033.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.033.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10,3.2) & ≈f(9,3)-0.033 \\\\ {e}^{\\sqrt{10}-3.2} & ≈{e}^{\\sqrt{9}-3}-0.033 \\\\  & ≈1-0.033 \\\\  & ≈0.967.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
