    "q-79876": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79876",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Estimate $(1.06)^{4.1}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{y}$ at the point $(1,4).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,4),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =4\\cdot {1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{1}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈4\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n(1.06)^{4.1}=f(1.06,4.1),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1.06,4.1).$\n\nMoving from $(1,4)$ to $(1.06,4.1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.06-1=0.06, \\\\ Δy & =4.1-4=0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈4\\cdot (0.06)+0\\cdot (0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.24+0 \\\\  & ≈0.24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.24.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.06,4.1) & ≈f(1,4)+0.24 \\\\ (1.06)^{4.1} & ≈{1}^{4}+0.24 \\\\  & ≈1+0.24 \\\\  & ≈1.24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
